
New functions and interfaces added beyond ixemul 48.2
=======================================================

The following functions and interfaces extend ixemul with additional libc,
POSIX, BSD/GNU and mathematical functionality. Entries include ixemul.library
system calls as well as header-only interfaces where explicitly noted.
No AmigaOS-specific extensions are provided unless explicitly stated.


64-bit integer conversion
=========================

strtoll(), strtoull(), atoll(), strtonum()
    Standard 64-bit integer conversion functions. strtonum() follows the
    OpenBSD interface, including range checking and error reporting. All
    functions operate entirely inside ixemul without AmigaOS interaction.


Allocation
==========

reallocarray()
    Overflow-checked allocation helper from OpenBSD. Performs safe
    multiplication of nmemb * size before calling realloc(). No memory
    pool integration is performed.

posix_memalign()
    POSIX aligned-memory allocation interface implemented on top of ixemul's
    existing memalign(). Validates alignment, returns POSIX error numbers
    directly, preserves the caller's errno value, and leaves *memptr unchanged
    on failure.


Stdio extensions
================

asprintf(), vasprintf()
    OpenBSD-style allocated formatted-output interfaces. vasprintf() first uses
    ixemul's vsnprintf() count-only path, allocates the exact required buffer,
    and then formats the result. asprintf() is the variadic wrapper around
    vasprintf(). Both return the formatted length on success and -1 on failure.


Time
====

nanosleep()
    Implements POSIX nanosleep(). Resolution is limited by AmigaOS timer
    device granularity. Sleep may be interrupted by signals and return
    EINTR as expected.

clock_gettime()
    Provides CLOCK_REALTIME and CLOCK_MONOTONIC. CLOCK_REALTIME uses
    gettimeofday(), while CLOCK_MONOTONIC uses the AmigaOS E-Clock through
    ReadEClock() and is not affected by changes to the system wall clock.

clock_getres()
    Reports the resolution of CLOCK_REALTIME and CLOCK_MONOTONIC.
    CLOCK_REALTIME reports microsecond resolution. CLOCK_MONOTONIC derives
    its resolution from the actual E-Clock frequency returned by ReadEClock().

clock_nanosleep()
    Implements POSIX clock_nanosleep() for CLOCK_REALTIME and
    CLOCK_MONOTONIC. Supports both relative sleeps and absolute deadlines
    through TIMER_ABSTIME. Relative CLOCK_MONOTONIC sleeps use the monotonic
    clock when calculating remaining time after EINTR. As required by POSIX,
    errors are returned directly as error numbers rather than through -1
    and errno.


Integer operations
==================

bswap16(), bswap32(), bswap64()
    Standard byte-swap operations identical to BSD/GNU implementations.

fls()
    Find-last-set bit. Returns the index (1-based) of the highest set bit.


Bitstring operations
====================

bitstring.h
    BSD bitstring compatibility interface provided as header macros rather
    than ixemul.library system calls. The implementation is based on the
    Berkeley bitstring API as carried by OpenBSD/NetBSD.

bitstr_size()
    Returns the number of bytes required to store a bitstring containing
    the requested number of bits.

bit_alloc()
    Allocates a zero-initialized bitstring using calloc().

bit_decl()
    Declares storage for a bitstring with the requested number of bits.

bit_test(), bit_set(), bit_clear()
    Test, set or clear an individual bit.

bit_nclear(), bit_nset()
    Clear or set an inclusive range of bits.

bit_ffc(), bit_ffs()
    Find the first clear or first set bit. The resulting bit index is
    returned through the caller-provided pointer, or -1 if no matching
    bit exists.


Memory operations
=================

explicit_bzero()
    Secure zeroing function. Uses volatile operations to prevent compiler
    optimization and guarantees memory is overwritten.

memmem()
    Searches for a byte sequence within another buffer.

mempcpy()
    Copies memory and returns a pointer to the end of the destination
    buffer. Matches glibc semantics.


String operations
=================

strcasestr()
    Case-insensitive substring search.

strlcpy(), strlcat()
    Safe string copy and concatenation functions from OpenBSD. Return
    values follow OpenBSD semantics (total length of the string attempted
    to be created).

stpcpy()
    Copies a string and returns a pointer to the terminating null byte.

strndup()
    Allocates and copies up to n bytes from a string.

strnlen()
    Returns the length of a string up to a maximum limit.

memrchr()
    Searches backward through a bounded memory block for the last occurrence
    of a byte value. The comparison uses unsigned-byte semantics and the
    function returns NULL when no matching byte is found.

strnvis()
    Size-bounded BSD vis(3) string encoder. Writes at most siz - 1 bytes,
    NUL-terminates the destination when siz is non-zero, and returns the
    complete encoded length required for the input string.

stravis()
    BSD vis(3) convenience interface that allocates the destination buffer,
    encodes the NUL-terminated source string, and returns the encoded length.

strnunvis()
    Size-bounded BSD unvis(3) string decoder. Decodes a NUL-terminated
    vis-encoded string, stores at most sz - 1 bytes when sz is non-zero,
    NUL-terminates the destination when possible, and returns the complete
    decoded length or -1 for an invalid escape sequence.

popcount32()
    Counts the number of set bits in a 32-bit unsigned value. The ixemul
    implementation is ported from NetBSD and uses its 32-bit population-count
    algorithm.

popcount64()
    Counts the number of set bits in a 64-bit unsigned value. On ixemul's
    32-bit m68k target the value is split into two 32-bit halves and counted
    through popcount32(), matching NetBSD's 32-bit-platform implementation.


Pathname operations
===================

mkdtemp()
    Creates a unique directory from a writable pathname template using
    ixemul's existing _gettemp() machinery. Returns the supplied pathname on
    success and NULL on failure.

realpath()
    Canonicalizes a pathname. Uses ixemul's virtual filesystem layer.
    Symbolic links and relative components are resolved according to
    ixemul semantics.

basename(), dirname()
    Pure string operations implementing POSIX basename() and dirname().
    These functions do not access the filesystem.



RFC2553 resolver additions
==========================

inet_ntop()
    Converts an IPv4 address from binary network format to presentation
    format. Writes the result to a caller-provided buffer and returns
    ENOSPC if the buffer is too small. The current ixemul implementation
    supports AF_INET only.

inet_pton()
    Converts an IPv4 address from presentation format to binary network
    format. Accepts strict decimal dotted-quad notation and leaves the
    destination buffer unchanged when the address is invalid. The current
    ixemul implementation supports AF_INET only.


Host/network byte order conversion
==================================

htonl(), htons(), ntohl(), ntohs()
    Standard host/network byte-order conversion functions. On big-endian m68k
    host order is already network order, so the ixemul implementations use the
    minimal NetBSD m68k assembler paths. The 16-bit variants explicitly
    zero-extend the return value.


Signal description
==================

strsignal()
    Returns the signal description from ixemul's existing sys_siglist table for
    valid signal numbers. Out-of-range values are formatted as
    "Unknown signal: N".


Descriptor polling
==================

poll()
    BSD-style descriptor polling implemented through ixemul's existing
    f_select() readiness protocol. Supports millisecond timeouts, POLLIN,
    POLLOUT and POLLPRI readiness, per-entry POLLNVAL reporting, duplicate
    pollfd entries, and pipe POLLHUP/POLLERR conditions.


File locking
============

flock()
    BSD advisory whole-file locking interface. Supports shared locks (LOCK_SH),
    exclusive locks (LOCK_EX), unlocking (LOCK_UN) and non-blocking operation
    through LOCK_NB. Lock ownership is associated with ixemul's open file
    object, and held locks are released when the final reference to that file
    object is closed.


Unix domain socket support
==========================

AF_UNIX / AF_LOCAL SOCK_STREAM
    Provides functional Unix domain stream socket support through ixemul's
    existing socket interface. Supports pathname-based bind(), listen(),
    accept() and connect(), bidirectional stream I/O, shutdown(), socket name
    queries, select() integration and limited SOL_SOCKET option handling.
    Datagram sockets are not supported, and connection timeouts are not
    implemented.


Mathematical header additions
=============================

expf(), exp2(), exp2f(), log2(), log2f(), log10f(), roundf(), truncf()
    C99-style mathematical additions provided by the current <math-68881.h>
    when the 68881-compatible FPU header path is selected through
    __HAVE_68881__. These functions are implemented as static inline
    definitions and are compiled into each translation unit that uses this
    header path. Their presence in <math-68881.h> does not by itself imply
    that matching external symbols are exported by ixemul.library or exist
    in the separately linked libm.

    The CPU option and FPU option are separate: for example, -m68060 alone
    does not select this header path, while -m68060 -m68881 does in the
    GCC 2.95.3 environment verified for ixemul.


